--- 4. BUILDABILITY BOUND ---
  --retire-batch 5 and 6 do not merely run slower, they cannot be emitted at all:
  RuntimeError: channel o queue is full of held tasks
  A token every rb-th task leaves runs of rb-1 untokened tasks, and the emitter demands a
  retirement as soon as a channel holds queue_depth (4, the shim start queue) of them, so
  rb >= queue_depth + 1 deadlocks a channel with more than queue_depth tasks.

--- 5. READ ---
  Both models have an interior optimum and they differ: SESR M7 at 2 (4.311) is 0.94 ms
  better than the 2026-09-17 default of 4 (5.254); YOLOv8n peaks at 3 (7.175) and at 2 reads
  7.336, i.e. 0.058 ms off today's 4 - inside the between-sitting drift this repo warns about,
  while SESR's 0.94 ms is 30x that drift and monotone across four cadences. rb=1 (a token on
  every task) is worse than rb=2 on SESR (4.573) at 165 kB of instructions against 145 kB,
  so awaits are not free: the U-shape is real. Default moved to 2; --retire-batch exposes it;
  every container records the cadence it was built with in its manifest.
